# rules.txt - StackChan reactive plugins. One rule per line, '#' = comment.
# Hot reload: the console's Rules section, or POST /api/rules/reload.
# Edit it from the console: Files tab > Configurations > rules.txt.
#
# FORMAT
#   enable | field | op | value | sustainMs | cooldownMs | action | a1 | a2
#
#   enable      GATE: the rule sleeps while THIS field is < 0.5.
#               Empty = always active.
#   field       what is watched. Published by the robot itself:
#                 batt chg rssi cam night mic micL micR light dark_sleepy ip
#               Anything else is yours: POST /api/field?name=value
#   op          gt  ge  lt  le  eq  ne
#   value       the threshold, a number
#   sustainMs   the condition must HOLD this long before firing (0 = at once)
#   cooldownMs  minimum delay between two firings of the same rule
#   action      SetEmotion <name> [ms] | PlayDance <name> | Blink
#               | WinkLeft | WinkRight | AmbientDark <0|1> | set <field> <val>
#
# HELD EDGE. A rule fires ONCE, when its condition becomes true and stays true
# for sustainMs. It re-arms only when the condition goes false again - so a
# field parked above its threshold does not spam the robot.
#
# SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION. A rule can only post whitelisted commands: it cannot
# draw on the screen and it cannot brick the robot. The reflexes (shake ->
# Scared, lift -> Curious) outrank every rule. And an unknown field reads 0,
# so a rule written for a source you do not run simply never fires.
#
# LINE LIMIT 127 BYTES. A longer line is DISCARDED whole, not truncated.
#
# =========================================================================
# CLAUDE CODE - THE CONTEXT GAUGE.  These four are LIVE, not examples.
# =========================================================================
# They are gated on `claude`, and only the statusline bridge pushes that
# field (scripts/dev/claude-statusline.ps1, or the .sh twin on Linux). Until
# you install it, `claude` reads 0 and none of them can fire. Install it and
# they need nothing else - the same script pushes `ctx`, the percentage of
# the context window in use. It sets `claude` to 1 ONLY while it can
# actually compute `ctx`, so these never fire on a missing number.
#
# Watch it on the band too: mode Gauges (console, or POST
# /api/statusbar?mode=3) shows g0 with the CTX label the bridge sets.
#
# Getting full: a first warning, well before it hurts.
claude | ctx | ge | 75 | 3000  | 120000 | SetEmotion | Worried | 4000
# Nearly full: this is the one worth reacting to.
claude | ctx | ge | 90 | 3000  | 60000  | SetEmotion | Scared  | 4000
# About to compact - the robot shakes its head, once every five minutes.
claude | ctx | ge | 97 | 2000  | 300000 | PlayDance  | shakeNo
# Fresh window, room to work. Held ten seconds so a compaction
# does not read as celebration.
claude | ctx | lt | 20 | 10000 | 600000 | SetEmotion | Happy   | 3000
#
# --- More to copy, all inactive (leading '#') ------------------------------
# Battery under 15 %, at most once a minute:
#  | batt | lt | 15 | 0 | 60000 | SetEmotion | Worried
# A loud noise startles it (needs the microphone on):
#  | micL | gt | 0.95 | 0 | 4000 | SetEmotion | Surprised | 1500
# Your own field, pushed by anything: POST /api/field?build=1
#  | build | ge | 1 | 0 | 5000 | PlayDance | nod
